<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 U04 L16 Multiply Numbers Larger than 20"><h2>Original Number Talk</h2><p>3×10=30,3×20=60,3×50=150,3×25=75.</p><h2>Clare and Andre: 4×23=92</h2><p>Original Clare had eight tens and twelve ones; original Andre4×23 area grid. Both92.</p><h2>Diego's original A–D partition choices</h2><div><strong>A: 12+11 columns</strong><div role="img" aria-label="4 by23 rectangle, partitioned12 and11" style="display:flex;width:460px;height:80px;border:1px solid;margin:6px 0"><span style="width:52.17391304347826%;border-right:1px dashed;display:grid;place-items:center">48</span><span style="flex:1;display:grid;place-items:center">44</span></div></div><div><strong>B: 15+8 columns</strong><div role="img" aria-label="4 by23 rectangle, partitioned15 and8" style="display:flex;width:460px;height:80px;border:1px solid;margin:6px 0"><span style="width:65.21739130434783%;border-right:1px dashed;display:grid;place-items:center">60</span><span style="flex:1;display:grid;place-items:center">32</span></div></div><div><strong>C: 10+13 columns</strong><div role="img" aria-label="4 by23 rectangle, partitioned10 and13" style="display:flex;width:460px;height:80px;border:1px solid;margin:6px 0"><span style="width:43.47826086956522%;border-right:1px dashed;display:grid;place-items:center">40</span><span style="flex:1;display:grid;place-items:center">52</span></div></div><div><strong>D: 20+3 columns</strong><div role="img" aria-label="4 by23 rectangle, partitioned20 and3" style="display:flex;width:460px;height:80px;border:1px solid;margin:6px 0"><span style="width:86.95652173913044%;border-right:1px dashed;display:grid;place-items:center">80</span><span style="flex:1;display:grid;place-items:center">12</span></div></div><p>All total92; D's tens-and-ones is generally easier, but all source partitions are valid. Independently drawn diagrams—not publisher SVG paths.</p><h2>Other original products</h2><p>3×28=84; Mai2×37=74;3×32=96;2×43=86;4×22=88;3×29=87.</p><h2>Optional Close to100</h2><p>Draw digit cards, choose two digits, make the closest product to100 over five rounds; original specifies this game as optional. Student-specific results are teacher observed. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-4/lesson-16/lesson.html">2021 original teacher source</a></p></section>
